---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import StatChip from '../../components/StatChip.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/figure/Figure.astro';
import ModalAuditBeforeAfter from '../../components/diagrams/ModalAuditBeforeAfter.astro';
import ApprovalFlow from '../../components/diagrams/ApprovalFlow.astro';
import QuotaCard from '../../components/diagrams/QuotaCard.astro';

const meta = [
  { label: 'Role', value: 'Senior UX Specialist — internal tools' },
  { label: 'Context', value: 'Ubisoft, Montréal' },
  { label: 'Timeline', value: 'May 2021 — Present' },
  { label: 'Scale', value: '1,000+ users' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Enterprise Systems at Ubisoft"
  eyebrow="Grouped page — three separate workstreams"
  heading="Enterprise Systems at Ubisoft — three workstreams in planning, approvals, and governance"
  subtitle="These are three distinct projects, not one. They share an environment — internal tools used by 1,000+ people, where staffing, remote-work, and hiring decisions actually happen — and they share a failure mode: systems people had stopped believing. Each got its own research, its own design, and its own fix."
  heroCaption="RPM: 40+ one-off modals collapse into one consistent, reusable pattern."
  meta={meta}
  seoDescription="Three Ubisoft internal-tools workstreams by Favour Diokpo — the RPM modal and ribbon revamp, five structured Work Arrangement approval flows, and warning-only Capacity Governance quota visibility."
>
  <ModalAuditBeforeAfter slot="hero" />

  <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-faint">
    Ubisoft UI is under NDA — this page uses redacted diagrams instead of screenshots. Every number
    comes from the source research documents.
  </p>

  <!-- Overview -->
  <section class="reveal mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">The environment</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <p>
        At <strong>Ubisoft</strong>, planning and staffing decisions run through internal tools used
        by more than <strong>1,000 people</strong> across production. Years of incremental additions
        had fragmented the surfaces: dozens of one-off modals, approval flows that only knew two
        answers, and quota rules nobody could see until they broke them. People didn’t stop working —
        they started routing around the tools. That’s the quiet failure mode of enterprise software.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal mt-8">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-4">
      <StatChip value="1,000+" label="Users across teams" />
      <StatChip value="40+" label="Modals audited" />
      <StatChip value="101" label="Survey responses" />
      <StatChip value="5" label="Approval flows defined" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Workstream 1: RPM ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="rpm" class="reveal mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Workstream 1</p>
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">RPM — modal &amp; ribbon revamp</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <p>
        <strong>The problem.</strong> RPM is Ubisoft’s main staffing platform. A prior redesign had
        changed the UI and workflows with no UX research, and 1,000+ users were living with the
        result. Complaints were constant, but nobody had mapped the causes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>My role.</strong> Sole UX designer. I owned the research, the audit, and the redesign.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What the research found.</strong> Rather than guess, I ran a global satisfaction
        survey — <strong>101 responses</strong> — followed by interviews. It overturned the obvious
        assumptions:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Seniority didn’t help.</strong> Long-time and frequent users were the <em>most</em> frustrated — time with the tool bred friction, not fluency.</li>
        <li><strong>Project Managers</strong> were the largest user group (38%) and among the least satisfied (3.3/5), so I ran deeper usability sessions with them.</li>
        <li>Overall satisfaction sat at <strong>3.5/5</strong> — but the tool scored <strong>2.16/5</strong> on saving people time, despite 62% of users being in it several times a week.</li>
        <li>Three themes repeated: too many steps for positions and requests, features people couldn’t find, and UI behavior so inconsistent the data itself looked wrong.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>What I designed.</strong> A 40+ modal audit collapsed into consistent, reusable
        patterns; a rebuilt navigation ribbon; and a focused redesign of the three core modals —
        Position, Request, and the Advanced Date Editor. I worked with Ubisoft’s shared design-system
        team (EGG) so developers shipped the exact components from design.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What it demonstrates.</strong> I can walk into a tool people resent, replace opinions
        with evidence, and fix the system instead of repainting the screens.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Workstream 2: Work Arrangement ───────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="work-arrangement" class="reveal mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Workstream 2</p>
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Work Arrangement — five structured approval flows</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <p>
        <strong>The problem.</strong> The tool employees use to formally request long-term remote work
        supported exactly two outcomes: approve or reject. Escalations, corrections, and sending a
        request back for revision happened over email — or not at all. What looked like a UI cleanup
        was a broken conversation between roles.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>My role.</strong> Sole UX designer, and in practice the product strategist — I framed
        the problem, set priorities, and sequenced the phased delivery without a formal PO title. I
        researched across four organizational levels: employees, direct managers, other managers, and
        the directors/HR committee.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What I designed.</strong> Five explicit flows, each with clear ownership:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>“Did you speak with the employee?”</strong> — a required checkpoint before any decision publishes, so a human conversation happens before the automation does.</li>
        <li><strong>Manager as final approver</strong> — the default path, with a templated, human notification instead of a silent status flip.</li>
        <li><strong>Send back to employee</strong> — a revision loop that replaced “cancel and resubmit.”</li>
        <li><strong>Escalate to committee</strong> and <strong>escalated-then-returned</strong> — separating who decides from who publishes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        HR policy constrained what managers could write, so free-text comments became
        <strong>legally-vetted templated messaging</strong> — clear for employees without exposing the
        company. The constraint didn’t kill the human solution; it reshaped it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What it demonstrates.</strong> I can design decision flows inside hard policy and
        legal constraints — and treat those constraints as material, not obstacles.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8">
      <Figure label="Approval flow with visible status and ownership at each stage." tag="Flow">
        <ApprovalFlow />
      </Figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Workstream 3: Capacity Governance ────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="capacity-governance" class="reveal mt-16 scroll-mt-24">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Workstream 3</p>
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">Capacity Governance — warning-only quota visibility</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <p>
        <strong>The problem.</strong> Quota rules that decide hiring were buried modals deep and easy
        to misread across studios. Rules that govern real money and real headcount were effectively
        invisible until someone tripped over them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What I designed.</strong> A <strong>warning-only</strong> quota model — used, buffer,
        threshold, time scope — surfaced at the point of decision. Over-cap teams are flagged in
        place, in dense grids that stay readable at real data volumes. The model informs; it never
        blocks. People route around walls, but they act on clear signals.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What it demonstrates.</strong> I can make dense, high-stakes data legible, and I know
        when governance should be a warning instead of a wall.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8">
      <Figure label="Quota visibility as a dense-but-legible surface — over-cap teams flagged in place." tag="Annotated UI">
        <QuotaCard />
      </Figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What they had in common -->
  <section class="reveal mt-16">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">What the three had in common</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <p>
        Each tool “worked” — the screens rendered, the buttons clicked. The real failures were
        underneath: nobody could see why a decision happened, who owned the next step, or which rule
        they were about to break. Research made those failures visible; systemization fixed them.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8">
      <Callout>
        The same instinct now runs through HERA: make the system tell the truth about its own state,
        and people stop routing around it.
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- What this proves -->
  <section class="reveal mt-14">
    <h2 class="text-h2 font-semibold tracking-tight">What this proves</h2>
    <div class="prose-hera mt-4">
      <ul>
        <li>I can run research at enterprise scale — a 101-response survey, interviews across four organizational levels — and turn it into a phased roadmap.</li>
        <li>I can systemize sprawl: 40+ one-off modals into reusable patterns a design-system team could ship.</li>
        <li>I can design approvals and governance under real HR and legal constraints.</li>
        <li>I can drive product decisions without a formal PO title — framing, priorities, and sequencing across product, engineering, HR, and operations.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
